changelog.done:
Partners now have one home: bitbaum. OrangeCat's partners page listed an OrangeCat guild that was never founded, so it showed an empty list while bitbaum had the real one. It now takes you straight to bitbaum's partners (https://bitbaum.orangecat.ch/partners/), where independent engineers apply and the studio approves who is listed.
Decide where money paid to you goes, in order. On Your money, under "Where your money goes", list your wallets in the order they should be covered: a share of everything that comes in (taxes first, say 25%), then amounts to fill — once, like paying back a debt, or every month, like rent or health insurance. When someone pays you, the whole payment goes to the first line still behind, and the page shows how far each line has got and where the next payment will land. OrangeCat never holds the money: the payer's wallet pays that wallet directly, and with no rule, payments land where they always did. {#money-routes}
A deck, made in the room. In your investor room, open Deck and generate a first draft from what the room and your product already say: the problem, the solution, live proof that it is real and moving, your own sections, the numbers, the roadmap and the ask — one claim per slide, nothing invented. Then edit any slide (its layout, words, figures, order), choose dark or light and your accent colour, and present it full screen with speaker notes. Investors open it from the room's Open the deck, swipe or arrow through it, link to a single slide, and save it as a PDF with real text. The proof slides read live data every time the deck is opened, so its numbers are never stale; opening the deck counts like any other open. {#room-deck}
Wallets for taxes and for paying back debt. Two new purposes when you add a wallet: Taxes — money set aside for the tax you owe — and Debt repayment — money going back to someone you owe. Like rent or medical wallets, they appear on your profile with their purpose.
Every payment remembers what it was worth when it arrived. When a payment to you is confirmed, OrangeCat now records the Bitcoin price at that moment in Swiss francs, euros, dollars and pounds. If no trustworthy price is available at that moment, it records nothing rather than a guess.
The investor room shows the evidence itself. Open a room and the first screen now answers "is it real, and is it moving": when the product went live, how many changes shipped in the last 30 days, how many were merged to the main branch since it started, and where the roadmap stands — each with its source, generated from the product's public repository and its own records rather than typed by anyone. Below: why the product exists (problem, solution, mission, vision), a week-by-week shipping chart with what recently shipped, and the roadmap by now / next / later. A row of links jumps between sections, a new room's outline asks for the risks, and the page tells the reader plainly that the owner sees what is opened. {#room-evidence}
A product's own investor page leads into its room. Heidi's /investors password now opens Heidi's investor room here. {#room-door}
An investor room on every project. The owner of a project now has a private page for the investors they choose: a headline, the story in sections (the problem, what was built, proof, market, business model, team, roadmap, the ask), dated numbers with how to check each, the deck, documents, and the project's build record from Loki. Each person gets their own link — no account, no password — that says who it is for, and the owner sees, per person, when they opened the room and what they opened inside it. A link can be switched off at any time; its history stays. Open it with "Investor room" in the bar at the top of your project's page. {#room-links}
Who opened what, counted honestly. The first time each person opens their link to an investor room, the owner gets a notification (and an email, which can be turned off). Link previews don't count: pasting a link into WhatsApp, Telegram, Slack or iMessage makes the app fetch the page for its preview card, and those fetches are no longer counted as someone opening it — in investor rooms and on profile claim links, where they were. Nor is the owner's own preview. {#room-opens}
"Came in" on Your money counts each sale once, at what it was worth. From the first sale, a sale would have been counted twice — once as the payment and once as the order — and a year of income converted at today's Bitcoin price, so the income and the tax estimate built on it would both have been off. Each payment now counts once, valued at the price recorded when it arrived. A payment that arrived before prices were recorded is valued at today's rate, and the page says how many were.
A payment that timed out is no longer recorded as failed. When your wallet doesn't answer in time, the Send screen already tells you the payment may still go through. OrangeCat's private record of the send now says the same: "unconfirmed", not "failed", because the money may have moved.

An event page built for whoever opens it. Most people arrive from a link in a group chat, on a phone, wanting three answers: when, where, am I in. Those now come first — the day and time on the venue's clock, the place, and the ticket — ahead of the description, which used to push them below the first screen.
Guests don't need an account. On a free event, "Get a free ticket" asks only for a name — no sign-up, no email. The page it opens is the ticket: the QR for the door, the night's time and place, and "Can't make it" to give the place back. The door list shows guests by the name they gave. So the link a host sends to the group chat works for everyone in it. {#event-rsvp-guests}
Raise money from one sentence. Go to /raise, say what you need ("a new roof for the community garden"), and the Cat prices it line by line, writes the title and story, and suggests how to raise it: backing, a loan, or investment. Every price is editable, a preview shows the page as backers will see it, and publishing gives you the link, a QR code and a share button. It works before you sign up; your plan waits for you after sign-in.
Back the road. Under /roadmap, three cards show how to support OrangeCat in the open: fund it, lend to it, or invest, each with what has been raised so far from the public ledger.
Tell your Cat about an event and it sets the whole thing up. A birthday, a meetup or a two-day conference: say what you're planning and the Cat asks what it needs in one message — what and when, where, how many and whether it's private or public, and who pays — then does it. The event page, free or ticketed; a wishlist so people chip in for the actual drinks, gear or venue deposit; a message to each guest or speaker on OrangeCat; and a reminder for you before the day. A public event also gets its announcement drafted, and the Cat can find a venue, caterer or DJ offered on OrangeCat and book the one you choose. Anything that publishes, books or messages someone waits for your okay.
An event from one sentence, mapped, with its crew. Say "electronic music night at Espresso Bar in Landquart on Friday at 10, techno and house, I need a DJ, two bartenders and someone on the door, 15 CHF" and, after you confirm, the Cat makes the whole event: the address on the map, the music and the vibe on the page, the price in your currency, and the crew posted as open roles. Say "post it" and it goes live. {#event-one-sentence}
Find what's on near you. /events has "What's on near you": share your location or type a place, pick the music and a distance, and get upcoming events nearest first. Or ask your Cat ("any house parties near Zürich this weekend?"); it answers from the same search. {#events-nearby}
Event times are the venue's time. A 22:00 night in Landquart reads 22:00 everywhere: on the page, in lists, at the door, on the venue's page and in what the Cat tells you, whoever reads it and wherever the server is. The Cat now writes "Friday at 10" as 10 at the venue, not 10 in UTC. {#event-time-zones}
Tickets with a QR, checked at the door. Paying for an event gives you a ticket on its page: a QR code, how many people it lets in, and a short code. Free events have "Get a free ticket", "Can't make it" gives the place back, and a full event stops selling before anyone pays. You get a notification with the ticket link. {#event-tickets}
A page for the place, with what's on there. "Make a page for my bar, Espresso Bar, Bahnhofstrasse 5, Landquart" sets up the bar as a place on the map, and its events show under "Happening here". If the bar isn't yours, name its owner and the page waits for them to claim it while you look after it. {#venue-pages}
The crew are real people, and the door crew check guests in. On your event page, put a person on a role by @username; they're notified and see "You're on the crew". Whoever holds the Door or Security role can open the door page and check guests in from their own phone. {#event-crew}
Pay your crew and refund tickets, on the record. Next to each person on the crew, "Pay" sends their fee in Bitcoin from your own connected wallet, or records that you paid them another way (Twint, cash). From the door list you can refund a paid ticket the same two ways; the ticket is cancelled and its place freed. Everything paid out shows on the event, nobody is paid twice for the same role, and a send that fails is recorded as failed. {#event-payouts}
Cover pictures. The event form has a cover picture, and every picture field on OrangeCat can now find an openly licensed photo or generate one with your own AI key, not only take an upload. Send the Cat a photo and ask for it as the cover, or ask it to make one; without an image key it creates the event anyway and tells you where to add one. {#event-cover}
The roadmap and the changelog point at each other. Each roadmap step that shipped links to the days in this changelog that delivered it, and each change here says which roadmap step it was for. The links come from the fleet's shared building-in-public kit, so the other products' pages can show them too.
The Cat keeps its instructions when the free model is full. A prompt too big for the free Groq tier was cut section by section — on "Throw a party on Saturday" twelve sections, the party plan and the tappable answers among them — and still did not fit, so Groq was skipped and the next model answered from what was left: an empty draft and one question. When cutting cannot make it fit and another model can take it whole, it now goes whole.
"Throw a party" gets questions, not an empty card. Tested against the live model: the Cat answered "Throw a party on Saturday" with a draft titled "Party on Saturday" — no date, no place — and a stiff list of what was "verified" and "unfinished". The step that picks the Cat's tools never saw the event playbook, so it drafted before the reply could ask. It now holds off until you've said when, where, roughly how many and who pays, then sets the plan up. When a reply runs out of time, the Cat says what it did in plain words instead of a report. {#event-one-sentence}
Signing in takes you where you were going. Opening a link like "ask the Cat: throw a party on Saturday" while signed out, then signing in (or tapping "Start instantly — no email"), landed on the dashboard and the Cat never got your sentence: the sign-in page sent you back to the link, and a second redirect sent you to the dashboard and won. Both now go to the link.
Mail to OrangeCat arrives. The FAQ, status, security, privacy and terms pages and the API description gave hello@, support@, security@ and integrations@orangecat.ch, and none of them was a mailbox, so a security report or a privacy request bounced. They all give cato@orangecat.ch now.
The careers page no longer lists jobs that do not exist. It showed four full-time openings with salaries and benefits; it now lists the real ways in — the open-source code, the partners' guild, and backing in Bitcoin. The footer says the code is MIT-licensed instead of "all rights reserved", and /technology names the framework versions the app actually runs.
A published investment page opens for everyone. Publishing an investment marked it open, but its public page only showed active ones, so a freshly published offering was a 404 for everyone but its owner, and its Invest card said "not open yet". It now shows as soon as it is published.
The Cat can create events again. Every event the Cat tried to create was refused: it wrote the place to a field events do not have and left the currency on a value the database rejects. The place now lands in the venue address, and the Cat can also set the kind of event, an end time, free entry or a ticket price, and how many people fit.
Published events open for everyone. The public event page only looked for listings marked "active", a status events never have, so a published event opened only for its organizer. Link previews and search results said "Event Not Found" for the same reason. {#event-one-sentence}
Buying an event ticket works. Checkout read a price field events don't have and refused every ticket; it charges the ticket price now. {#event-tickets}
"Near me" can find you. The site told browsers not to share location with itself, so "Near me" could never work. When location can't be had, it now says why — blocked, unavailable or too slow — instead of one sentence for all three. {#events-nearby}
Booking requests, booking updates and review reminders reach you. Their notifications were refused by the database and only logged, so nobody saw them. Every kind of notification the app sends is now on one list the database checks against.
Asking the Cat to register something you own works. It left out the owner and the kind of thing, so every attempt failed.

Citations in the law chat are written so they link. The chat's answers cite provisions as [L3]; the instruction now says no spaces inside the brackets, which one model had been adding. {#law-ask}
"And in Lucerne?" keeps its subject. When a follow-up question has no subject of its own and the model cannot plan from it, the whole conversation is planned as one question, with the place named last added. {#law-ask}
A weak search plan gets a second opinion. When the model that plans a search names no act, or only repeats the words of the question instead of putting them into the law's language, the next model in the chain is asked once more, so one unlucky model no longer decides whether a rule is found. {#law-ask}
The law chat starts from the act a lawyer would open. The model now says which statutes or ordinances probably govern a question (it points; it does not answer), and the chat searches those acts first with the question's ideas, then reads what they say. One question asked twice no longer depends on which words ranked. {#law-ask}
The law chat looks wider before it chooses. It now considers up to fifteen provisions per place and lets the model pick the few that apply, so a rule the words rank twelfth is still found. {#law-ask}
Search puts the fundamental act first. A short ordinance that repeats the question's words no longer outranks the statute that makes the rule, and a search that would take too long is cut off instead of holding the database. A fixed set of questions (pnpm run check:law-search) measures whether the right provision is found. {#law-ask}
The law chat checks its own search. After searching, it reads the headings of what it found, keeps the provisions that bear on the question, drops the ones that only share a word, and searches again for an obvious rule that is missing (the permit, the canton's own act). {#law-ask}
Search finds a provision by its ideas. The law search and the law chat now ask for provisions that contain every idea of a question first (the animal and the permit), then the main idea alone, instead of ranking a common word like "permit" above the rare one that matters. The chat answers in the language of the question. {#law-ask}
You can ask the law a question. /law/ask is a chat, built on the same chat as Loki and OrangeCat, open to everyone and in any language. It works out which places the question is about ("and in Lucerne?" keeps the fox from the question before), searches those places' provisions, and answers only from them, each statement linked to the provision it rests on. Search can also take several authorities at once. {#law-ask}
The City of Zürich's own laws can be loaded, with their history. A reader for the city's Amtliche Sammlung takes each act's versions with the days the city states, and any other commune joins by adding one reader. The loading tool takes municipal --publisher zurich-city, and --history adds the earlier versions. {#law-communes}
The text of the law can be searched. GET /api/v1/law/search?q= ranks the provisions in force that match, optionally by authority or language, and each hit names the act, the article, the publisher's page and the hash of the copy, so an answer built on it can cite exactly what it read. {#law-search}
Laws can be found and read on Solon. A new page, Find a law, searches the register by title or number and by whose law it is; each act opens with its provisions, a link to the publisher, the page the copy was read from and its hash. A version the publisher does not date says it was read on that day. {#law-reading}
Zürich's laws can be read from the canton's own collection. The loading tool takes each Zürich act's current version from ZH-Lex, the canton's PDF, with the day the canton says it took effect, and retires the first-pass reading of the same act. The constitution comes in at 139 articles, in force since 1 January
Liechtenstein's laws are in the register. The 3,407 acts in force in the Government's Systematic Register (statutes, ordinances, notices, decrees and treaties) are listed with their LR number, their title and the Landesgesetzblatt issue that published them, beside the Swiss ones on /law/coverage and at /api/v1/law/acts?authority=li. Names and numbers only; the text from gesetze.li comes next. The communes are not listed yet. (#283) {#law-register-li}
Solon can read Liechtenstein's laws from gesetze.li. The loading tool reads each act's consolidated page, article by article, with the page it came from and a hash that proves the copy. Lilex does not say since when a version applies, so each is dated by the day it was read and marked as such. Production is not loaded yet. (#284)
Every Swiss act can point to the place that publishes it. The register can now keep, for each act of the Confederation and the cantons, the address of its page at its own authority, and /api/v1/law/acts returns it as officialUrl. Production is not loaded yet. (#284)
Cantonal laws can be read as text. The loading tool reads each cantonal act's document and cuts it into articles, with the document it came from and a hash that proves the copy. This is a first pass, dated by the day it was read; the cantons' own publications will replace it canton by canton. Production is not loaded yet. (#284)
A register of the law. Solon now lists every act of the Confederation, the 26 cantons and the intercantonal agreements: 33,441 acts, 22,047 of them in force, each with its title and its official number, read from LexFind's list and counted per authority on /law/coverage. Acts can be found by authority, number or title at /api/v1/law/acts. Only names and numbers so far: the text of the laws comes next, publisher by publisher. Liechtenstein and the communes are not listed yet. (#281) {#law-register}
Solon can hold the text of the law. The Confederation's acts can now be read from Fedlex, article by article, with the page they came from and a hash that proves the copy, and looked up as they stood on any day a held version covers (/api/v1/law/provisions). The loading tool is built and tested on real federal acts; production is not loaded yet, so /law/coverage still shows no text held. (#282)

Value routing to originators. originator_share v1: 10% of a product's net revenue, by default, to the originators of the code it is built from — equal per originator, monthly, in Bitcoin, on a public ledger. The split is deterministic and anyone can recount it; every later version needs an approved allocation-policy vote. (#164) {#originator-share}
One signature step for claiming a seat and filing a proposal. (#165)
FleetCrown is now called Loki everywhere on the site. (#157)

The agent reads the law itself. For Swiss federal law it now opens the article on Fedlex, the official collection, and reads the text in force today before it states the rule. The answer cites the article with its link. This needs no search engine and no paid service, so it works when web search does not. Liechtenstein law is read the same way, from gesetze.li, the Government's consolidated collection, and the law of all 26 cantons through LexFind, from each canton's own official text. {#read-law}
A quick check opens as a matter. Under the deadline, one tap moves the check into a sealed matter. The deadline and its sentence, the letter, the draft reply and the place come along. What the check hid from the model stays hidden. Signed out, it goes through sign-in and comes back to the check. {#letter-to-matter}
Ask across documents. In a matter, ask up to three questions of every document at once: who pays, when it ends, what notice applies. The answers come back as a table, one row per document, and every answer quotes its passage. An answer whose quote is not in the document is dropped rather than shown. The table is saved to the matter as a draft. {#document-review}
Answers come in the reader's language. After reading German statutes the agent once answered an English question in German. An answer plainly in another language than the reader's is now sent back to be written again, keeping its facts and citations. {#answer-language}
A turn no longer ends with "I could not finish". Free models misbehave in ways that a plain answer check does not catch: one reply asked for the same statute a hundred and fifty times, another wrote its answer as a tool call in the last round, where no tool can run. A reply now runs at most eight calls and a repeat only once, a turn at most twelve law readings and eight searches, a turn with nothing to show gets one more round, with tools off, by another model, and the best real answer of the turn is kept when a later reply is only a note of a call. {#turn-never-empty}
Search works again, on our own server. The search engine on the host had four sources, all four blocked for too many requests or a CAPTCHA, so every search came back empty. More free sources are switched on and a block lasts minutes instead of hours. The other engines still refuse the host, so search stays thin and the law is read from the publishers instead. No paid service is involved. {#search-again}
The agent researches before it states the law. A real question on the live site was answered from memory: no search, no source, and the tool calls it should have made written into the answer as text. Every answer is now checked: one that cites law without a source, describes calls instead of making them, or gives a deadline without recording it, is sent back once (up to twice, each time to the next model in the chain); if it still fails it is shown marked "unverified". {#no-unsourced-law}
The roadmap records where this is going. The law will be read from Solon as data, with the publisher as the check; the problems people bring most often (a company, a divorce, a notice ending a tenancy, choosing a vehicle for an investment) are named as workflows to build; Luxembourg and Ireland are named as the next readers; and the lawyer credential is described as what it is, a signed selective disclosure and not yet a zero-knowledge proof. None of this is built. {#roadmap-direction}

A new front page. It shows instead of telling: an invented letter worked through (the name hidden, the deadline found with its source, a draft, your decision), the days a letter usually costs against the minutes here, the anonymisation drawn as it happens, and an honest comparison with a general chatbot and a lawyer. Headlines are set in sentence case. {#front-page}
Italian. The whole site, the account screens and the emails are now also in Italian. A letter can be translated into nineteen languages, from Albanian to Tigrinya. {#more-languages}
Switzerland and Liechtenstein. Matters and helper profiles can be set in Liechtenstein as well as in any canton, and the agent is told what is particular to each legal order. {#markets}
Choose on sign-up whether you need help or give it. Both are the same account. {#sign-up-role}
Start from a common problem. Debt collection notice, rent increase, tax assessment, dismissal, insurance refused, founding an association: one tap tells the agent what has to be established and what to draft, and tells you what to have ready. No law is written into them: the agent finds and cites the rule and its deadline each time. "Find a helper" from such a matter opens the directory on its area. {#workflows}
Helpers you can find and ask. A directory of legal assistants, licensed lawyers and AI services, with Causius AI first. Search forgives a typo; filter by kind, country, language and area. Nobody is invented: every profile is a real account that listed itself. {#helpers}
Become a helper. Any account can list itself, under a name or a pseudonym, and still keep matters of its own. "Create a helper account" sits on the sign-up page. {#helper-desk}
A lawyer checked, and still pseudonymous. A lawyer submits their admission privately; a Causius reviewer checks it in the public register. The public then sees a signed attestation (SD-JWT, Ed25519) that names the place, never the person, and anyone can check it at /verify or with the key at /.well-known/jwks.json. A lawyer is listed only where it was checked. {#attestations}
Ask a helper about your matter. You choose what they see: without your personal details (the default) or everything. Before sending you read your request exactly as they will, and can hide a name the patterns missed. A lawyer who accepts gives you the name Causius checked, with proof it is that name. Ending it ends their access at once. {#engagements}
The account menu's label no longer shows a formatting error to screen readers. {#account-label}

A look of its own: ink on paper. Causius is light by default, deep ink-indigo on warm paper, with headlines set like an inscription; dark mode follows your device or the switch in the header, and your choice is kept in this browser. Amber and red stay for deadlines and problems only. {#look}
Much less to read. The home page went from about a thousand words to about two hundred and forty: what it is, how it works, why it is private, who can help. What is real today and the identity statement moved to the platform page; the quick check, the case page, the helpers page and the security page were cut by a quarter to a third. {#less-to-read}
Header and footer are easier to hit. Language, theme and footer links are all at least 44 by 44 pixels, and nothing runs off the edge of a phone. {#nav-contract}

Say it, photograph it, attach it. Starting a matter and writing in one use the same message box: a microphone, a camera/photos/files menu, and a choice of model. Photos are covered by you in the browser before they are read; PDFs and text files are added as they are. {#intake}
Speaking, disclosed. A spoken take goes to a speech service and cannot be hidden first. The text comes back for you to edit, and the security page says so. {#voice}
Hiding personal details is sturdier. Titles (Dr., Prof.), initials, ALL-CAPS names, street and PO-box forms, phone and IBAN variants are now caught, and a very long paste can no longer slow the check down. {#redaction}
The agent is stricter with what it reads. Web pages and documents are treated as untrusted text, the agent refuses internal addresses, and one matter handles one message at a time. {#agent-hardening}
The security page says only what is true. Deadline dates are readable in the database, detection is not perfect, and the feedback widget is named. {#honest-security}

Matters, worked by an agent. Sign in with your OrangeCat account and open a matter: Causius researches what applies and cites it, records deadlines and tasks, drafts documents, and proposes anything that would leave the matter (sending, filing, signing, paying) for you to approve. {#matters}
Sealed on our host. Each matter is encrypted with its own key; the database alone holds no name, message, deadline or document. Your account name is always among the words hidden from the model. {#sealed-matters}
Documents in a matter. Add a PDF or a text file; only its text is kept, encrypted. {#documents}
Domain names checked with the registries. Before recommending a name for something you are founding, the agent asks the registries whether its domains are registered. {#domains}
One account across the fleet. "Create an account" opens OrangeCat's screen; the same account opens OrangeCat, Solon and Loki, and the account menu links to them. {#sign-in}
The fleet's look, and the story behind it. Causius now wears the shared design of OrangeCat, Loki and Solon, with new pages on how it is built, how it keeps matters private, and where it sits in the fleet. {#narrative}
Your details never reach the model. Before a letter is sent, your browser replaces names, addresses, AHV and IBAN numbers, phone numbers, e-mail addresses, dates of birth and reference numbers with placeholders. You see every replacement, can add words of your own, and can preview exactly what is sent. The real values go back into the result on your device. {#anonymise}
Cover details on a photo. Black out your name and address on each page before it is sent; what is under the box is removed from the image. {#anonymise-photos}
An anonymised case file for helpers. Export a case with your details replaced, to hand to a legal assistant or a lawyer. {#anonymise-export}
A strict security policy. The browser runs only scripts the page vouches for, sends data only to Causius, and refuses framing. {#security}
English pages answered with an error. Every page now lives under its language (/en, /de, /fr), and / opens in your browser's language.
Causius speaks English, German and French. The whole interface, and what an analysis says about your letter, in the language you choose in the header. German follows Swiss spelling. [#7] {#languages}
Letters of several pages. Photograph up to four pages in order. Photos are made smaller in your browser before they are sent, so a phone picture is no longer too large. [#7] {#pages}
The deadline shows its source. Under the reply date is the sentence of the letter it was read from, to compare with your own copy. A date the letter does not state is never worked out for you. [#7] {#deadline-source}
You are told before you start. The intake page says whether the free models are on, and whether they can read photos, before you add a letter. [#7] {#shared-models}

Causius reads a letter. Paste an official letter or add a photo of it and get a case: the deadline with the days left, each thing asked of you as a checklist, the letter in the languages you chose, and a draft reply to fill in. [#7] {#intake} {#case-file}
Your case stays with you. Cases are kept in your browser only. Export one as a file to hand to a helper or move to another device, and import it again. [#7] {#export}
Your own model. Connect an API key from one of ten providers. The key is checked as you paste it, the strongest model it can use is preselected, and it is kept in your browser only. [#7] {#own-model}
Free shared models, fairly shared. Without a key, letters are read by the fleet's shared free models, a few per hour per visitor. A photo goes only to a model that can read one. [#7] {#shared-models}
Helpers, and an honest line between them. What a legal assistant may do, what only a licensed lawyer may do, and who may work under a pseudonym. Nobody is listed yet, and the page says so. [#7] {#helper-rules}
Roadmap and changelog. Both are kept in the repository and shown on the site from the fleet map. [#7] {#records}

Keep your file in your account, if you choose. One switch in Account. The file is kept sealed on our server and follows you to any device you sign in on. Off by default; turning it off deletes the copy.
Ask about your own file. Questions answered from your records only, with names hidden before anything is sent. Information, not a diagnosis.
A health file, not a report reader. The header is now Today, Records and People. Today shows what is next, the latest results and your medicines; Records is everything in one list you can search (a typo still finds it) and filter, and you can add medicines, appointments and notes beside reports. The home page says so in fewer words.
Accounts, if you want one. Sign in with email and password, a link, a passkey, two-factor, OrangeCat or the shared sign-in, in all five languages. Reading a report still needs none, and nothing health-related is stored on the server.
Much less to read. The home page went from about a thousand words to a couple of hundred: what it is, one value read, how it works, why it is private, and who can help. The depth moved to Platform and Security; the intake form, the report page and People were cut by a quarter to a third. A test now holds each page to a word budget. {#less-text}
Warm paper and pine green, not clinical blue. Blue is every hospital's colour. The look is one file (app/theme.css), so it stays a one-line change. {#colour}
Every control in the header and footer is at least 44px, at phone, tablet and desktop width, in all five languages. {#targets}

Read a lab report. Paste it or photograph its pages, in any language. Every value comes out with its unit, its printed range and the line of the report it was read from. {#read}
The numbers decide, not the model. Whether a value is outside its range is worked out by comparing it with the printed bounds, and the urgency shown can never be lower than the values imply. {#flags}
What it means, as information. A plain explanation for each value outside its range, questions to take to your doctor, and the words of a non-lab document explained. Never a diagnosis. {#explain}
Follow a value over time. When the same measurement is in an earlier report in this browser, the earlier value and the direction are shown. Units are never converted. {#trends}
Private by construction. Names, birth dates, addresses, insurance, patient and order numbers are replaced in your browser before anything is sent; reports live in this browser only; an anonymised file can be exported for a clinician. {#privacy}
Five languages. The whole site and every reading in English, German, French, Italian and Spanish. {#languages}
Your own model. Connect an API key and the reading runs on your own budget; the key stays in your browser. {#own-model}
Roadmap, changelog and the rules for clinicians, said plainly, with nobody listed who does not exist. {#records}

Three divisions, five pillars, and what did not work.
evig is stated as three divisions — computers, technicians, ai — from one list that drives the wordmarks, the navigation, the footer and the division pages (#300, #389). {#divisions-one-list}
evig repairs became evig technicians: post a request, get offers from technicians nearby, choose one; evig stopped asking for printers (#382). {#evig-technicians}
The homepage states the five pillars instead of a donation funnel, and no longer says the same thing three times (#381, #385). {#five-pillars}
The "not a charity" line is gone — it contradicted the legal page; evig architecture and evig health moved to their own projects (#379). {#not-a-charity}
The donate buttons funded another organisation; they fund evig now, and two navigation entries that went nowhere were removed (#388).
The address printed on the site did not receive mail; it does now (#376).
Invoices charged the pre-2024 VAT rate; one rate is used everywhere (#368). Paid marketplace listings no longer stay reserved forever, and the order page stopped showing a CHF 0.00 service fee (#369, #373).
Eight dialogs trap focus and close with Escape (#372).
Money routes require the finance permission, cron routes deny by default, and a vote is cast by whoever proves it rather than whoever types an email address (#305, #322, #358). The one live decision can be voted on again (#308).
Staff can fill the category form from a sentence and then change it by talking to it (#279).

Revamp-IT becomes evig.
New name, identity and canonical address: evig.orangecat.ch (#247, #248, #256).
Every page says what is true: evig is a Verein in Gründung, not a recognised non-profit. {#verein-in-gruendung}
The invented track record, metrics and testimonials are gone, and so are the donation-receipt and charity claims evig cannot make yet. {#no-false-receipts}
The physical store address, opening hours and Revamp-IT's projects and social accounts were removed — evig is not Revamp-IT.
Feedback goes through the fleet's embeddable widget.

Checked before it is sold.
A device whose required test fails cannot reach the shop; staff can still publish without QC in one audited click, and the listing then carries no Prüfsiegel so buyers see it is untested. {#qc-skip}
Final QA keeps the four-eyes rule, with an audited one-click solo override. {#four-eyes}
Every CO₂ figure comes from per-category ADEME open data, minus 15% for refurbishment, floored at 5 kg; categories without a defensible factor show no claim. {#co2-open-data}
The methodology is public at /transparenz/co2 and linked under "Über uns". {#co2-methodology}
Removing a listing returns the device to the pipeline, and the pipeline shows what is live in the shop.
